# What this machine is called, declared — because the name is what `binding`
# matches on, and matching is how a checkout learns which profile it wears.
#
# THIS IS INTENT, NOT A MIRROR. macOS keeps these in the SystemConfiguration
# store, not in a file anybody may link. The machine is asked with `scutil`;
# this file is what the answer is supposed to be.
#
# THREE COLUMNS, AND THE FIRST IS THE PLATFORM. macOS has a `ComputerName` and
# Windows has a `COMPUTERNAME` and they are not the same field — one is a
# display string with spaces and an apostrophe in it, the other is a
# NetBIOS-flavoured label. Sharing a key would let one host match the other's
# name. See `../binding`.
#
# UNFILLED ON PURPOSE. Nobody has stood in front of editing bay 1 and asked it
# what it answers to. Until somebody does, `machines/binding` matches nothing
# here and reports this checkout as unclaimed — which is correct, and is the
# clean state rather than a fault.
#
# To fill it, on the machine:
#
#     scutil --get LocalHostName
#
# and put the answer below. Do not guess it from the sticker on the front.

# platform   key             value
# darwin     LocalHostName
